so one of the the most interesting  things that I found and that was most  surprising to me really was in my  interviews with egg donors and sperm  donors it became very clear that the  sperm donors had a very straightforward  view of themselves as fathers to the  children who are born as a result of  their donations whereas the women were  absolutely adamant that they were not  mothers and that's so surprising because  women and men each provide half of the  genetic material that you need to create  an embryo and so the question is well  why would they have such different ways  of thinking about their relationship to  their biological offspring and I think  that it really comes back to in our  society how we define what makes a  mother and what makes a father and as a  result of reproductive technologies the  definition of what makes a mother is  much more flexible there are different  women who can contribute to one  particular child so there can be one  woman who provides the egg one woman who  carries the pregnancy another woman or  women who raise the child  and so in that sense our definitions of  motherhood can be sort of partitioned  out into different elements whereas our  definitions of fatherhood are not quite  so flexible we still have a cultural  equation in which providing a sperm  makes a man a father and so it's it's  very difficult for sperm donors to say  something that I heard from egg donors  over and over sperm donors would not say  well all I did was give just the sperm  so I'm not the father  whereas egg donors were just you know to  a person I interviewed women in  different parts of the country who  donated through different donation  programs and I heard over and over and  over while all I did was all I gave was  just an egg and they can say just an egg  and I'm not the mother because they  weren't carrying the pregnancy they  weren't giving birth they weren't  raising the child so they were  distancing themselves from that label of  mother in part because of how technology  has changed how we define families  I didn't do research on surrogate  mothers or women who were the recipients  of egg donations for example or women  who are mothers through old-fashioned  means of reproduction but research by  other scholars really demonstrates that  the flexibility of these definitions so  if we took you know I was talking about  egg donor saying well it's just the egg  I didn't carry the pregnancy I didn't  give birth I didn't raise the child  people who interview surrogate mothers  find that surrogate mothers will say  well all I did was just the pregnancy I  didn't give the egg I'm not raising the  child and so surrogate mothers will  distance themselves from that label of  mother and so that's where I'm pointing  to the flexibility in our definitions of  maternity of what it is that makes a  woman a mother that the person who  provides the egg the person who provides  the pregnancy the person who raises the  child any one of those women could lay  claim to that label of mother or not  depending on her eventual intentions of  parenting that child  where we have seen some of the most  controversial court cases in assisted  reproduction have been of surrogacy so  there was the baby M trial in 1987 there  was another legal decision in California  Johnson V Calvert that came a few years  later and both of those were around the  death of who should be identified as a  parent I mean in the United States right  now in general the fertility industry  and assisted reproduction it goes pretty  much unregulated and so we have a  state-by-state system where some states  ban surrogacy some states allow  surrogacy some states allow surrogacy  but don't allow it to be compensated and  the same thing with egg donation in  sperm donation there's sort of different  regulations around who around how these  technologies work depending on what  state you're in in our country and so  that produces a situation that is very  difficult to to keep track of and it  actually is in pretty stark contrast to  many European countries where there is  far more regulation around donation and  anonymity and regulation around  compensation so we in the United States  have very few regulations compared to  most other industrialized countries  I don't know that I would necessarily be  in favor of more regulation I think that  you know recently there have been  stories in the news of a sperm donor  with a hundred and fifty children or  some of these these court cases around  surrogacy that I've talked about and  every time one of these sort of  scandalous stories about the fertility  industry comes out the first call that  you see in the newspapers is for more  regulation more regulation and I think  that's understandable but I would take a  step back and say well first of all we  actually need more information about how  the fertility industry works at this  point there is no national body that  collects statistics about how many egg  donors and sperm donors there are in the  United States about how often they  donate about how many children are born  from egg or sperm donations about what  happens to those children so we just  have just an absolute dearth of  information about how the industry  actually works so I would argue that  before we can regulate we have to know  what's going on and so my call would  actually be for more more data more  information about how it works
 

